\i \cf0 You might as well. I promise you, there is nothing inside that will harm you.
\i0  The voice of the crown said.\
Markal stretched his hand out gingerly and touched the stone. It was cold. The surface was absurdly smooth for stone, and very precisely cut. He could feel the razor sharpness of the edges of the incised designs. It could have been cut yesterday. He pushed, gently. Nothing happened.\

\i Why won\'92t it open?
\i0  he asked.\

\i Because you\'92re not doing it right. Look to either side. See the depressions?
\i0 \
Markal looked, and indeed there were small areas on either side of the door, at about shoulder height. They were indentations, roughly the size of a human hand. In the centre of each was a tiny spike, a miniature obelisk of this strange stone, pointing straight out.\

\i I should put my hands on the spikes?
\i0 \

\i It\'92s the only way you\'92re getting through the door. It\'92s a blood lock. Only certain people can open that door. The blood lock restricts access to certain groups. It\'92s how my people prevented yours from accessing their deepest secrets.
\i0  The voice said.\

\i I\'92m not an elf! It won\'92t work for me!
\i0 \

\i You\'92d be surprised, boy. I don\'92t have time to explain now, just trust me.\
What choice do I have?
\i0  Markal asked. There was no answer, just a ghostly chuckle. He gritted his teeth in frustration. Adjusting his stance, he put his hands in the indentations in the stone. As he pushed, the spikes pricked his palms, so sharp he barely noticed, and the door began to swing. Once moving, it seemed to proceed of its own accord, opening wide to reveal the chamber beyond.\
Markal had to shield his eyes. The light was blinding, seeming to come from everywhere at once. He could just make out a large room, every surface dazzlingly white. As his eyes adjusted he became aware of more details. Lining the walls were recesses in which stood items he could only assume were weapons. In the centre of the room was an enormous statue. It was a tall, slender figure in flowing robes, holding a smooth staff in one hand and a fantastically long, single-edged Aelfin sword in the other. On his head was a familiar crown.\

\i Yes, that\'92s me. For the record, he\'92s not that impressive in person. He could barely hold that sword. He wasn\'92t half the elf his father was. I suppose his son is on the throne by now. I wonder what he\'92s like.
\i0 \
Markal just stared at the crown on the statue\'92s brow, unable to think, all reason banished by that alabaster image.\

\i It\'92s the Aelfin King, isn\'92t it?
\i0  He finally managed. 
\i The crown\'85 you\'85 are the crown of the Aelfin King?\
One of them. He had several. They aren\'92t really a symbol of office for the Aelfin. That usage is peculiar to your race. You saw them on the heads of your masters. Once your masters were gone, you kept the custom. They are tools, that is all. Each useful according to its nature.\
And your nature is?\
Control. Of the elements. Of nature.\
Of us?\
