\i0 \cf0 The voice was silent. Markal let it drop. Looking around the room he could see his companions investigating the recesses and the weapons in them. Most were obvious analogs of weapons with which Markal was familiar. There were pikes, axes, flails, crossbows, but there were also other things. Some were built like a crossbow, but with no obvious projectile, or means of launching one, others were like nothing Markal had ever seen. One was a simple rectangular slab of what looked like onyx, another looked like nothing so much as a large bell made of glass. On the wall to the left of the statue was a familiar looking sword. It was exactly like the one the king\'92s statue held. Fully six feet long from pommel to tip, it was a grand weapon, but as Markal took it down and felt its perfect balance he knew he had to take it.\
The chamber curved around them in a large ellipse, with doors at each point. The far door, beyond the statue, lay open, but beyond it was more darkness. Only this room was illuminated. Looking up, Markal could see why. In the ceiling were holes, venting sunlight down to this chamber, and prisms and mirrors reflecting it around so that it lit the whole space. It was a startlingly effective design, and Markal thought it might one day make a fabulous centerpiece for the palace at Aegarshalle, should he ever make it back there.\
Away at the far end, Karlin was already inspecting the room beyond. Markal went to join him, passing Peter on the way. The pirate was attempting to stuff as much as he could carry into a makeshift sack he\'92d apparently made from the tattered remains of his cloak. Markal grabbed his arm. \'93You might want to be careful what you take from here. These are ancient weapons of Aelfin design. Who knows what half of them do. A person might make a mistake and blow his own leg off\'85\'94 Peter gulped and put some of the items back immediately.\
Liesbet stood with Karlin and some of the others at the far door, still holding her hammer. \'93You didn\'92t want to take anything?\'94 Markal asked.\
\'93Didn\'92t see anything I liked. Too refined for me. Give me a nice heavy hammer any day.\'94 By way of demonstration she weighed the head of the hammer in her palm. Markal shrugged.\
Beyond the doorway the space opened out again. This cavern was larger than the first one, and had been filled with what appeared to be a small town. There were elegant buildings everywhere. There were no holes in the ceiling here, but there were small globes mounted on poles that glowed with a sickly green light that was at least enough to see by even if it did little else. The floor of the cavern was laid with cobbles, and dotted with what passed for subteranean gardens. Strange species of fungi and odd coral-like plants grew here, wild within their own spaces, but never intruding upon the cobbles, as if prevented by a sheet of glass.\
